# Routh I.Art.158 Ex.1 — three projectiles stay in a plane parallel to itself
# — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "A Treatise on Dynamics of a Particle" (Cambridge, 1898),
# Part I, Art. 158, Ex. 1: "If three heavy particles be projected
# simultaneously from the same point in any directions with any velocities,
# prove that the plane passing through them will always remain parallel to
# itself."  [Math. T. 1847.]
#
# THE PROOF, IN ONE LINE. Art. 154 is the whole chapter: gravity is fixed in
# direction and magnitude and independent of position, so every particle obeys
#
#     r_i(t) = P + V_i*t + (1/2)*g*t^2
#
# with the SAME (1/2)*g*t^2 for all of them. Subtract any two:
#
#     r_i - r_j = (V_i - V_j)*t
#
# and the gravity term cancels identically. The two edge vectors of the
# triangle are therefore (V1-V2)*t and (V1-V3)*t, so their cross product is
#
#     t^2 * (V1-V2) x (V1-V3)
#
# -- a FIXED direction. The plane's normal never turns, so the plane merely
# translates. The same cancellation also shows the triangle stays similar to
# itself, since both edges scale by the same factor t.
#
# THE NUMBERS. All three start at the origin under gravity (0, -9.81, 0), with
#
#     V0 = ( 3, 8,  1)      V1 = (-2, 6,  4)      V2 = ( 1, 9, -3)
#
# so V0-V1 = (5, 2, -3) and V0-V2 = (2, -1, 4), whose cross product is
# (5, -26, -9). Normalised, the plane's unit normal is
#
#     (0.17879963496303788, -0.929758101807797, -0.3218393429334682)
#
# for all t > 0. The cells print that normal at t = 1, 2 and 4: three identical
# vectors, while the triangle itself has grown fourfold and fallen 78 units.
#
# SETUP NOTE. system.g_constant is set to 0 so the particles do NOT attract one
# another -- posim's pairwise gravity is on by default with G = 1, and Routh's
# problem is about a uniform field only. With no pairwise term the three may
# share a starting point with no singularity. At t = 0 the triangle has zero
# area and its normal is undefined, so the first sample is taken at t = 1.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: three particles fired on wildly different paths, falling
# together. The triangle they span swells steadily but never tilts.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p1_three_projectiles
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_three_projectiles.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_three_projectiles.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.gravity = [0, -9.81, 0]
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new point { mass = 1, position = [0, 0, 0], velocity = [ 3, 8,  1] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0, 0, 0], velocity = [-2, 6,  4] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0, 0, 0], velocity = [ 1, 9, -3] }

run 1 steps 1
normalize(cross(obj1.position - obj0.position, obj2.position - obj0.position))
run 1 steps 1
normalize(cross(obj1.position - obj0.position, obj2.position - obj0.position))
run 2 steps 2
normalize(cross(obj1.position - obj0.position, obj2.position - obj0.position))
# the triangle has grown but not turned: one edge length at t = 4
norm(obj1.position - obj0.position)
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.gravity = [0, -9.81, 0]
new point { mass = 1, position = [0, 0, 0], velocity = [ 3, 8,  1] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0, 0, 0], velocity = [-2, 6,  4] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0, 0, 0], velocity = [ 1, 9, -3] }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.02
